            "round"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.6,
      "why": "Full pipelining gives 1 result/cycle, so Fmax alone must be >= 1 MHz; the critical path is a single micro-rotation, so it is the most likely family to meet the throughput constraint while keeping area low. Narrowing n_iter to 10-16 and data_width to 12-16 focuses on the accuracy/area sweet spot where max_abs_err <= 2^-10 is achievable with minimal area."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 16,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 10,
          "high": 16,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null,
          "choices": [
            "round"
          ]
        },
        {
          "param": "m",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "Registering every m stages (m=2-4) reduces flip-flop count and area versus full pipelining, at the cost of a longer critical path (m micro-rotations per stage). This trades area for Fmax, which is exactly the trade-off the spec cares about; it may yield smaller area designs that still meet the 1 MSPS throughput floor."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands >= 1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area as the sole objective. Only pipelined and pipelined_m produce 1 result/cycle, so they are the only families that can meet the throughput constraint without relying on high Fmax. I focus data_width on 12-16 and n_iter on 10-16 to cover the accuracy sweet spot where the error constraint is met with minimal area. Rounding is fixed to 'round' to minimize error for a given width. pipelined gets 60% of budget as the baseline area reference, and pipelined_m gets 40% to explore the area-reduction trade-off from fewer registers. This should map the Pareto front between area and accuracy_bits effectively within the 100-eval budget."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The Pareto front is well-established with 5 feasible designs covering the area range 21355-40143 um^2 and accuracy 10.5-11.8 bits. The minimum area design (pipelined_m, 21355 um^2) meets all constraints: throughput 45.8 MSPS >= 1 MSPS and max_abs_err 0.000673 <= 0.000976562. The front spans a meaningful trade-off curve with good hypervolume (2.506e+04). Since this is the final round, the current front is sufficient for selection via min area_um2, which picks the 21355 um^2 pipelined_m design."
  ,
  "next_plan": null
}
